      },
      "lineNbr": 0,
      "author": {
        "id": 1125653
      },
      "writtenOn": "2025-01-22T12:29:35Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "But sysfs is NOT the current bluetooth user/kernel api, why is this ok to do it this way for JUST this one function?  Just add it through the normal user/kernel api instead of placing random sysfs files in userspace with no protection from users breaking things.",
      "parentUuid": "74334492_6f51cac8",
      "revId": "c2fa49ca594db6637bc67ea13d8915c6578bd6c5",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "6e721fd1_8ce3c61e",
        "filename": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 0,
      "author": {
        "id": 1962300
      },
      "writtenOn": "2025-01-22T12:35:18Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "There is no so-called \"normal\" user/kernel api for Bluetooth. The MGMT api is used for BlueZ\u0027s userspace part to communicate with its kernel space part, and it\u0027s just not suitable with other usecase. We don\u0027t use BlueZ in Android.",
      "parentUuid": "8ebcd2a5_a6c0a2d5",
      "revId": "c2fa49ca594db6637bc67ea13d8915c6578bd6c5",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "435a40a3_d1ebad73",
        "filename": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 0,
      "author": {
        "id": 1125653
      },
      "writtenOn": "2025-01-22T12:44:31Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "But you use the same user/kernel api that bluez uses, otherwise how can you control bluetooth devices?  What\u0027s wrong with just adding another function there?\n\nAdding a single sysfs api feels very wrong, why not just follow how all othter bluetooth control interfaces work?",
      "parentUuid": "6e721fd1_8ce3c61e",
      "revId": "c2fa49ca594db6637bc67ea13d8915c6578bd6c5",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "4accca71_1d37146e",
        "filename": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 0,
      "author": {
        "id": 1962300
      },
      "writtenOn": "2025-01-22T13:11:56Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "We control the bluetooth device through the HCI_USER_CHANNEL raw socket, compose / serialize the packets in userspace and the kernel just forwards it to the chip.\n\nWe can\u0027t export this function through that socket because the protocol is defined in the Bluetooth core specification. Moreover, this sysfs reset function aims to recover the Bluetooth chip in a out-of-band way when the socket is not responding e.g. due to the chip firmware issue.",
      "parentUuid": "435a40a3_d1ebad73",
      "revId": "c2fa49ca594db6637bc67ea13d8915c6578bd6c5",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "c3765603_84d4e3c1",
        "filename": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 0,
      "author": {
        "id": 1125653
      },
      "writtenOn": "2025-01-22T14:22:04Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "What about all of the custom \"non spec\" ioctls in `hci_sock_ioctl()`?  Why not add a \"reset device\" one there?",
      "parentUuid": "4accca71_1d37146e",
      "revId": "c2fa49ca594db6637bc67ea13d8915c6578bd6c5",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
